WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.064 --> 00:00:02.120
(upbeat music)

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.721
- [Jenna] This season of Travel
Dares finds us on the road

00:00:04.721 --> 00:00:07.503
to reconnect with the US
and what makes it unique.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:09.860
On this episode,

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.210
I'm visiting a place known
as the country's oldest

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:14.353
native American artists' cooperative.

00:00:14.353 --> 00:00:16.936
(upbeat music)

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:21.140
Last episode,

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.557
I got a clue for where I'd
end up on this episode.

00:00:23.557 --> 00:00:25.430
"Use your fingers to handcraft

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:27.510
a piece of pre-Colonial history."

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:28.960
Which led me to Asheville,

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.230
a city nestled in North Carolina's famous

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.120
Great Smoky Mountains.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.400
But I'll actually be traveling
just outside the city

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.860
to the town of Cherokee.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.540
Where I'll be visiting the
Qualla Arts and Crafts Mutual.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.050
So I'm going to wrap up breakfast now

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.300
and head on over there.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.501
Thank you so much.

00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:46.084
(upbeat music)

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.800
Heading to the Qualla Arts
and Crafts Mutual today,

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.550
I hear it's their 75th year anniversary.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.886
I'm really excited.

00:00:54.886 --> 00:00:57.210
(upbeat music)

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.610
- Hi, nice to meet you.
Jenna: Nice to meet you, I'm Jenna.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.850
Tonya: I'm Tonya, welcome
to Qualla Arts and Crafts.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.290
Jenna: Thank you for having me.

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:05.730
I'd love to know a little
more about why the cooperative

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.038
was founded and when it was founded.

00:01:08.038 --> 00:01:10.872
Tonya: The cooperative was founded in 1946

00:01:10.872 --> 00:01:13.337
by Cherokee artists,

00:01:13.337 --> 00:01:16.810
and they wanted to have a place
that they could sell their

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.260
artwork year round.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.660
We have a lot of different
mediums of artwork

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.030
at Qualla Arts and Crafts.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:25.040
We have traditional Cherokee baskets,

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.050
stone carvings, wood carvings.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:31.080
We also have contemporary
work like bead work, drawings,

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:35.110
paintings, prints, and even some textiles.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:36.810
The Qualla Arts and Crafts has over

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.950
250 artist members currently.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.733
A lot of the artwork is passed
down through family members

00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:44.640
through the home,

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.440
but also our school system has adopted

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.460
Cherokee arts and crafts as a class.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:51.990
So most schools have a STEM program,

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:55.160
our school system, we named it STEAM,

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.340
which adds the "A" in there for the arts.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:00.800
So it's science, technology,
engineering, the arts and math.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.640
Jenna: I love that.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.100
Tonya: So now we're
going to give you a taste

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.840
of some of the arts and crafts we do here.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:06.860
Are you ready Jenna?

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:07.693
Jenna: Oh, yeah.

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:08.573
I'm so ready.

00:02:10.047 --> 00:02:10.990
Jenna: Hi.
- Hi.

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:11.823
Jenna: What's your name?

00:02:11.823 --> 00:02:12.656
Tara: I'm Tara.

00:02:12.656 --> 00:02:13.489
Jenna: Tara, I'm Jenna.

00:02:13.489 --> 00:02:14.700
It's very nice to meet you.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:15.540
Tara: Nice to meet you.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.420
Jenna: I am dying to know about pottery.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.880
I'd love to know how you got into it,

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.652
how long you've been doing it?

00:02:22.652 --> 00:02:25.110
Tara: I started when I was
probably around seventh grade

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.390
and I stepped away from
it when I went to college.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:29.710
And then when I came back home,

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:32.603
I picked it back up and I've
been doing it since 1999.

00:02:32.603 --> 00:02:34.430
Jenna: That's a long time.
Tara: Mm hm.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:35.897
Jenna: I'm in good hands.

00:02:35.897 --> 00:02:36.955
(both laugh)

00:02:36.955 --> 00:02:38.530
Tara: Yes, I hope so.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.930
All right, so you can get your clay,

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:42.960
I already cut you a ball clay out.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.676
And so you just gonna pat it.

00:02:44.676 --> 00:02:47.357
(clay patting)

00:02:47.357 --> 00:02:49.890
So you just get it in a flat disc

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:51.390
and you're just gonna use your roller

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:54.181
and you're just gonna flatten it out.

00:02:54.181 --> 00:02:57.450
(soft upbeat music)

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.110
And then you're going to take this,

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:02.020
this is your base and
all we're going to do is,

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:03.740
we're just gonna lay it around

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:05.930
and you're just going to press it down,

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:09.383
form it to the bowl or the base.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.600
So now that our clay has dried,

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.970
we're gonna add some coils to it.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.360
You're gonna take a ball of clay

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.960
and all you're gonna do is
you're going to just start

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.545
squeezing it into like a snake.

00:03:25.545 --> 00:03:30.545
And all I do is I lay it on top of my pot.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:35.160
And at this stage I use my paddle

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.160
and all I'm doing is I'm gonna go around

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:39.833
and smack the clay with it.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.420
And so if you notice, as you're smacking,

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:46.533
it's getting thinner, the
clay is getting longer.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.280
And then once you get
to where you want to go,

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:52.930
once you get your decorations
on and you like the shape,

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:56.840
then I would let it dry
for about seven to 10 days.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:58.660
And then you can put it in a kiln.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:03:59.970
There's your pots.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.000
Jenna: Thank you so much for teaching me.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.250
A limited amount of time,
but I feel like I absorbed

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.089
hopefully at least a little bit.

00:04:07.089 --> 00:04:09.913
Tara: You should be able to
go home and make a pot now.

00:04:09.913 --> 00:04:11.843
(both laughing)

00:04:11.843 --> 00:04:14.426
(upbeat music)

00:04:17.526 --> 00:04:18.359
Jenna: Hey.

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:19.192
- Hi.

00:04:19.192 --> 00:04:20.230
Jenna: I'm Jenna.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:21.063
Bill: I'm Bill.

00:04:21.063 --> 00:04:22.920
Jenna: Hi Bill, what are you making?

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:24.020
Bill: A Turtle.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:26.400
I'm curving a turtle out of soapstone.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.099
Jenna: A turtle out of a soapstone?

00:04:28.099 --> 00:04:28.932
Bill: Mm hm.

00:04:28.932 --> 00:04:31.125
I tell kids my knife's magic,
'cause it'll cut a rock.

00:04:31.125 --> 00:04:32.740
(Jenna laughing)

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:34.390
Jenna: How long have you been doing this?

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:36.670
Bill: Off and on since 1986.

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:37.671
Jenna: 1986?

00:04:37.671 --> 00:04:38.504
Bill: Yeah

00:04:38.504 --> 00:04:39.855
Jenna: Okay, a long time.

00:04:39.855 --> 00:04:41.380
How'd you get started?

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:44.500
Bill: I got sick back in the
'80s and it wasn't able to work

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:49.500
for a couple of years and I
can't do very well at waiting

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:51.840
and sitting and stuff like that.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.670
So I started doing
something with my hands.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:58.490
Jenna: How do you become a
member here at the cooperative?

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:03.490
Bill: You have to show the
board that you can do the work.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:07.180
You have to present three
to five pieces of your work,

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:11.090
and then we will call you
in to demonstrate for us

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:13.440
so that we'll know you did the work.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:18.290
And if the work is the
quality that we want here,

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.980
then you can become a member.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:21.910
Jenna: Well, now I'm really nervous

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:24.050
because I want you to
show me a few skills,

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:26.244
but I do not want to be tested.

00:05:26.244 --> 00:05:28.140
(Bill laughs)

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:29.057
Bill: Okay.

00:05:29.057 --> 00:05:30.023
(Jenna laughs)

00:05:30.023 --> 00:05:31.360
We can work with that.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:32.960
Jenna: Your enthusiasm is contagious.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:35.353
Bill: Choose your weapon.

00:05:36.267 --> 00:05:37.840
I've got knives.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:39.390
Jenna: I feel like this one looks like

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:41.141
I'll do the least damage on myself.

00:05:41.141 --> 00:05:41.974
(both laughing)

00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:43.320
Bill: Okay.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:46.810
I'd just start trying
to check it out first,

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:47.863
to cut that stone.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.700
There's sometimes a
little hard spots in it,

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:53.758
sometimes it's smooth.

00:05:53.758 --> 00:05:55.808
Jenna: I'm just feeling
hard spots in general.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:00.540
What makes Qualla special in your opinion?

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:04.070
Bill: The fact that we are
trying our best to keep

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:05.910
a tradition alive.

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:08.210
These walls right here around us,

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:11.130
have objects in them that were made,

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.020
some of them many years ago.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.840
And you go out there
and look at the shelves

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.350
at the new work,

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:19.730
And some of it's almost exactly the same.

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:23.260
I wouldn't say it's exactly
because I can sit here

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:26.300
and carve hundreds of these turtles

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.290
and you could probably name them because

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:31.560
I doubt if I could
carve two exactly alike.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.340
In fact, I'm not sure I'd even want to.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:38.340
It's really showing the past
through the work we do today.

00:06:39.623 --> 00:06:40.963
Bill: Can I take a look at it?

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:44.780
Oh my gosh, yeah it's coming
to life right out of the...

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:46.850
Bill: Right out of that piece of rock.

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:48.740
Jenna: It's kind of amazing.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:51.710
Bill: People think rock's dead, not to me.

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:52.870
Jenna: I love that.

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:55.990
Bill: Rocks live, and I
try to bring them to life.

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:58.717
Jenna: Well, I feel so lucky to have met

00:06:58.717 --> 00:07:01.040
your living rocks and you.

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.300
Thank you for giving me
the opportunity to sit down

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.200
and chat with you and hear your stories.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:06.033
Bill: You're welcome.

00:07:06.033 --> 00:07:06.933
I'm glad you did.

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:10.500
Jenna: I had such an amazing time

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:11.870
meeting with the artists earlier

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:14.880
and seeing the level of expertise
and skill that goes into

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:18.500
their respective crafts was
such an eye opening experience.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:20.309
The Qualla Arts and Crafts Mutual

00:07:20.309 --> 00:07:22.570
is such a beautiful location.

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:24.860
And the fact that they've been
keeping the local Cherokee

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:28.840
traditional alive for the
past 75 years is amazing.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:29.930
I don't know about you,

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:32.210
but I think I'm ready
for my next adventure.

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:34.567
And I think I see a clue.

00:07:34.567 --> 00:07:35.980
"You'll have to go underground

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:38.380
to see this tall beauty in person."

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:40.520
Ooh, tall beauty in person.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:41.730
I have no idea what that means,

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:43.060
but I'm very excited.

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:44.840
You'll have to tune in to the next episode

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:46.023
to find out where I'm going next.

00:07:46.023 --> 00:07:48.606
(upbeat music)

